hi there Steve Kaufman here today I want  to talk about how we can make progress  or progress how do we progress in  language learning and to me the answer  is very simple  keep moving move forward that's what  progress means move forward advance let  me explain what I mean  we often get at link learners who come  on  and they tell me that they've been  listening to the same lesson 30 40 50  times and they've been doing this over a  period of months and they're trying very  hard you know to make sure that they  learn the lesson but there's still parts  of it that they don't understand and and  parts of it that they keep forgetting  and so forth and what should they do  well my answer is move on to something  else don't stay at one lesson and listen  to it 30 or 40 times you have to admit  you want to advance advance move forward  when I start in a language yes I will  listen to very simple content you know  less than a minute long 30 seconds long  and I listen to it 10 20 times maybe not  all at once like a lot of our beginner  lessons that link are part of a series  so there might be 20 of them so I might  listen to one of them twice for three or  four times then do the second one and  the third one and the fourth one always  listening to three four or five times  then go back to the first one again the  immediate challenge in a language is to  even pick out where you know one word  ends and the next word begins it's all  sort of indiscriminate noise at first  but as you progress and as you can at  least tell where the words begin or  previous word ends then you are now  ready to start moving a little more  quickly in my opinion and I say in my  opinion because you know we all have to  decide how we want to learn I can only  share with you  what works for me and what I think is  good and if I think it's good for me I'm  inclined to think it's good for other  people but there are lots of polyglots  who are very successful and who have  different methods and you should listen  to them all and find those things that  they have in common or those things that  work for you but I only talk about what  works for me and what I think is good  and I think it's good to listen a lot at  the beginning maybe for a month but not  twenty times on one lesson maybe have a  simple series of lessons which could be  from a beginner series like teach  yourself it doesn't have to be our  program at link and listen two three  four times to lesson one and then move  on to lesson two and do the same and  three and so forth you can always go  back you should go back you know I think  we have this idea in school that we kind  of go from lesson one to two to three  and if we don't lesson learn it while  we're on lesson one will never learn it  that's just not true you have to keep on  going back and in fact it's very  difficult to learn it while you're on  that lesson it's difficult to learn  anything no matter how hard you try like  you know as an example so many times you  know I'll think of a word in Russian  that I've heard many times and I'll say  I'll go and look it up now because I  really want to know what that word means  and I'll do that and I'll look it up but  I'll forget it again how many times have  I looked at the you know the declension  tables because I'm still struggling with  whatever and I go and look it up and oh  yeah okay now I know you know not a day  or two later I've already forgotten it  because you just need so much exposure  before these things start to become  natural for you so it's very important  not to stay with one lesson and one book  that I often mention which is how the  brain learns I think it's called  learning something do with the brain but  the writer of this book is a neuro  scientist called Manfred Spitzer from  Germany and there are two quotes that I  often use the first one was that  the brain in order to learn requires  repetition but it also requires novelty  once there's no more novelty the brain  stops learning it needs new things so it  needs basically a mixture of repetition  and novelty if you're staying with one  lesson and trying to force these things  into your brain the novelty aspect is  now gone diminishing returns the law of  diminishing returns sets in you're  learning less and less you can't force  yourself in that way  therefore it's far better to move along  progress advance move forward onto  something else because those words that  you're trying to learn if they're  important they will come up again  meanwhile you're stimulating your brain  hopefully with interesting content it's  something new and fresh the brain is now  turned on again to try to learn  something and everything it's so normal  to forget normal not to understand you  don't have to wait until you understand  you know a hundred percent of the lesson  I'll move on when I understand fifty  sixty percent but I always go back and  it's very nice when you go back and then  you understand more and and I think it's  also a good idea to very sort of more  challenging material often authentic  material with easy material so if you're  driven by an interest in political  events or history or literature or  whatever music to push yourself push the  barriers to go to more and more  difficult material you should  occasionally go back and do easier  material because when you look at  beginner material where you know all the  words you're able to focus on those  structures or problems that you have so  it's often a good idea to give yourself  a break every now and again and go back  and do easier material that was one of  the quotes from Manfred Spitzer we need  repetition and we need novelty new  things another quote was learning takes  place in the brain not in the classroom  and that's very important because a lot  of our traditional views about how we  learn are conditioned by our experience  in the classroom we have to have lesson  1 and then lesson 2 and that's lesson 3  learn it we're gonna learn for the test  and then we'll have it and of course it  doesn't work that way  countless generations of kids have gone  through school passing their tests and  French let's say English school system  in Canada and they graduate and they  can't speak so that the whole process of  learning all this stuff cramming it for  the exam may enable you to pass the exam  it doesn't enable you to learn the  language and if we look around the world  most people learn languages outside the  classroom those countries in Europe  where television programs and movies are  in the original language they are better  at foreign languages as a general rule  than those countries where they don't do  that if you go to Sweden or Holland they  all watch TV in English but there's no  less than one in the TV the kid starts  watching a cartoon series or whatever it  might be he's hit with the language and  that's gradually how his familiarity  with the language improves so of course  when you're learning you can be a little  more purposeful than that because you  can listen to things that you can also  read as in our lessons that link you can  look up words you can study the words  but don't stay too long on the same  material  keep moving yourself forward let's see I  had some notes here yeah the thing about  language too is unlike other skills you  know engineering medicine music these  are things that in a way we have to  learn a little more deliberately  although my wife and and my  granddaughter have progressed much more  in piano playing since they stopped  having to sit down with a teacher once a  week in other words they're motivated to  learn on their own they play and they  learn but still the thing about language  that makes it different is that language  surrounds us we can get this access to  language everywhere we can find movies  we can find radio we can find podcasts  that language surrounds us so it's not  quite the same as learning a specific  technique and and even they're like  credentials which comes up quite  regularly I think cred  initials in terms of a credential you  know a teaching credential and so forth  is quite irrelevant to language learning  or even language teaching there is no  correlation between the credentials of  the teacher and the teachers success  what matters is the teachers experience  ability to communicate to convey  enthusiasm not necessarily experience  but knowledge of the subject enthusiasm  can ability to encourage and so forth so  the credentials which govern you know  how to organize a class and some of the  things that are taught there it's not  bad but it doesn't necessarily determine  who is in a position to help you learn  because the learning is not just done in  a classroom so when you go out to try to  you know determine how you should go  about learning a language by all means  listen to what teachers have to say but  also check in on some of the other  people on the Internet and there are  thousands of them who are handing out  advice on how to learn language under no  means the only one but my advice for  today is if you want to move forward if  you want to progress in your language  keep moving forward don't get stuck  don't try to nail things down it's too  difficult to do eventually through this  exposure to more and more interesting  material to a mixture of repetition and  novelty the brain will start to acquire  the language so there you have it on how  to progress in language learning thank  you for listening bye for now
 

